Both cameras come out sideways because nobody declares how they are mounted

libcamera was warning about it and I had ignored it:

    's5k3t2 13-0010': Failed to retrieve the camera location
    's5k3t2 13-0010': Rotation control not available, default to 0 degrees

The device tree declares neither rotation nor orientation for either of them, and
the imx682 driver did not even read those properties -the s5k3t2 one does-.
Without that information libcamera assumes zero degrees and the application draws
the image sideways, which is exactly what is seen in the KDE camera app.

THE ANGLES ARE MEASURED, NOT DEDUCED

The phone was stood upright pointing at the room and a raw capture was taken with
both cameras on the same scene. Then each image was rotated in both directions
and the one that came out upright was checked:

    rear      90 degrees clockwise        -> upright. The text on a bag reads
                                            correctly, the mug stands up and the
                                            corner of the wall comes out vertical.
    front     90 degrees COUNTERCLOCKWISE -> upright. With the clockwise one the
                                            monitor appeared to hang from the
                                            ceiling.

That they come out opposite is what you would expect: they look in opposite
directions and are mounted mirrored.

THE SIGN COMES FROM THE IMPLEMENTATION, which is the only thing that rules here.
The binding description defines rotation as the COUNTERCLOCKWISE angle between
two frames of reference, and with those diagrams it is easy to get the sign
wrong. But libcamera converts it like this:

    orientationFromRotation(int angle)   /* "rotation of the given angle
                                            clockwise" */
        case 90:  return Orientation::Rotate90;

and Orientation::Rotate90 is EXIF tag 8, that is, "it must be rotated 90 degrees
clockwise to be seen correctly". Which is exactly what was measured on the rear
camera.

Moreover, since these drivers expose neither horizontal nor vertical flip,
computeTransform() returns the identity and limits itself to REPORTING the
orientation: it rotates nothing on its own, it is the application that
straightens the image. That is why declaring it correctly is enough.

THE SIGN, CORRECTED BY THE TEST IN THE APPLICATION

The first attempt put 90 on the rear and 270 on the front, and in the KDE camera
app both came out rotated by 180 degrees. Which is exactly the difference between
90 and 270, that is, the sign flipped.

The raw image measurement does not change and is still the one above. What was
wrong was translating it into the device tree number: the binding defines rotation
as the COUNTERCLOCKWISE angle between two frames of reference, whereas
libcamera's orientationFromRotation() documents its argument as clockwise. I went
with the function's convention and it was the other one.

Rear 270 and front 90 it is.

--- a/drivers/media/i2c/imx682.c
+++ b/drivers/media/i2c/imx682.c
@@ -1059,6 +1059,7 @@
 {
 	const struct imx682_mode *m = s->mode;
 	struct v4l2_ctrl_handler *h = &s->ctrls;
+	struct v4l2_fwnode_device_properties props;
 	int ret;
 
 	ret = v4l2_ctrl_handler_init(h, 8);
@@ -1118,6 +1119,29 @@
 			  IMX682_ANA_GAIN_MIN, IMX682_ANA_GAIN_MAX,
 			  IMX682_ANA_GAIN_STEP, IMX682_ANA_GAIN_MIN);
 
+	/*
+	 * ORIENTACION Y ROTACION.
+	 *
+	 * Sin esto libcamera avisa "Failed to retrieve the camera location" y
+	 * "Rotation control not available, default to 0 degrees", y la imagen
+	 * sale tumbada en el visor: el sensor esta montado girado respecto al
+	 * movil y nadie se lo dice a nadie.
+	 *
+	 * Los dos valores vienen del device tree, que es donde estan porque
+	 * dependen de la placa y no del chip.
+	 */
+	ret = v4l2_fwnode_device_parse(&s->client->dev, &props);
+	if (ret) {
+		v4l2_ctrl_handler_free(h);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	ret = v4l2_ctrl_new_fwnode_properties(h, &imx682_ctrl_ops, &props);
+	if (ret) {
+		v4l2_ctrl_handler_free(h);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
 	if (h->error) {
 		ret = h->error;
 		dev_err(&s->client->dev, "fallo al crear los controles: %d\n", ret);
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm7150-xiaomi-surya.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm7150-xiaomi-surya.dtsi
@@ -274,6 +274,28 @@
 		compatible = "sony,imx682";
 		reg = <0x1a>;
 
+		/*
+		 * MEDIDO, no deducido: se puso el movil de pie apuntando a la
+		 * habitacion y se miro la captura en crudo. La imagen del sensor
+		 * hay que girarla 90 grados EN SENTIDO HORARIO para que quede
+		 * derecha -el texto de una bolsa se lee, la taza esta de pie y
+		 * la esquina de la pared sale vertical-.
+		 *
+		 * EL SIGNO, CORREGIDO CON LA PRUEBA EN LA APLICACION.
+		 *
+		 * La medida de la imagen cruda es la de arriba y no cambia. Lo
+		 * que estaba mal era traducirla al numero del device tree: el
+		 * binding define la rotacion como angulo ANTIHORARIO entre dos
+		 * sistemas de referencia, mientras que orientationFromRotation()
+		 * de libcamera documenta su argumento como horario. Elegi el
+		 * criterio de la funcion y salio al reves.
+		 *
+		 * Con 90 la imagen aparecia girada 180 grados en la camara de
+		 * KDE, que es exactamente la diferencia entre 90 y 270.
+		 */
+		rotation = <270>;
+		orientation = <1>;	/* trasera */
+
 		reset-gpios = <&tlmm 25 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
 		pinctrl-names = "default";
 		pinctrl-0 = <&cam_mclk1_active>;
@@ -325,6 +347,17 @@
 		compatible = "samsung,s5k3t2";
 		reg = <0x10>;
 
+		/*
+		 * Medida igual que la trasera y con la misma escena. Esta va al
+		 * reves que la trasera: hay que girarla 90 grados en sentido
+		 * ANTIHORARIO. Es lo esperable, porque las dos camaras miran a
+		 * lados opuestos y van montadas en espejo.
+		 *
+		 * Mismo cambio de signo que en la trasera, por el mismo motivo.
+		 */
+		rotation = <90>;
+		orientation = <0>;	/* frontal */
+
 		/* CAM3_RST_N */
 		reset-gpios = <&tlmm 23 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
 		pinctrl-names = "default";
